---
import ToolLayout from '../layouts/ToolLayout.astro';
import JsonToXml from '../components/JsonToXml';
import RelatedTools from '../components/RelatedTools.astro';
import FAQ from '../components/FAQ.astro';
import AdSlot from '../components/AdSlot.astro';
import { getTool, getRelatedTools } from '../data/tools';

const tool = getTool('json-to-xml')!;
const related = getRelatedTools(tool);

const faqItems = [
  { q: 'How are JSON arrays represented in XML?', a: 'Each array item becomes a repeated sibling element, named after the array\'s key with a trailing "s" stripped (e.g. tools -> <tool>).' },
  { q: 'What happens to keys that aren\'t valid XML tag names?', a: 'Keys with characters XML tags don\'t allow (like spaces) fall back to a generic <item> tag.' },
  { q: 'Can I set the root element name?', a: 'Yes — the Root field in the toolbar controls the wrapping element name.' },
  { q: 'Is my data uploaded anywhere?', a: 'No — the conversion runs entirely in your browser.' },
];
---
<ToolLayout tool={tool}>
  <JsonToXml client:load />

  <AdSlot placement="below-tool" />

  <div class="mx-auto w-full max-w-[860px] space-y-8 px-4 py-10">
    <RelatedTools tools={related} />

    <details class="rounded-lg border" style="border-color:var(--border);background:var(--bg-subtle)">
      <summary class="cursor-pointer px-4 py-3 text-sm font-semibold" style="color:var(--fg)">Documentation</summary>
      <div class="space-y-5 px-4 pb-5 text-sm leading-relaxed" style="color:var(--fg-muted)">
        <section>
          <h2 class="mb-1.5 text-xs font-semibold uppercase tracking-wider" style="color:var(--accent)">What is JSON to XML?</h2>
          <p>This tool converts a JSON document into well-formed XML, wrapping the whole thing in a configurable root element. It's useful when a downstream system — a legacy API, an XSLT pipeline, a SOAP endpoint — expects XML but your data originates as JSON.</p>
        </section>
        <section>
          <h2 class="mb-1.5 text-xs font-semibold uppercase tracking-wider" style="color:var(--accent)">How it works</h2>
          <p>Each JSON key becomes an XML tag wrapping its value: objects recurse into child elements, primitive values become text content, and <code class="font-mono text-xs" style="color:var(--accent)">null</code>/<code class="font-mono text-xs" style="color:var(--accent)">undefined</code> become a self-closing empty tag (<code class="font-mono text-xs" style="color:var(--accent)">{`<field/>`}</code>) rather than an empty pair of tags. Arrays are handled specially: the array's own key has its trailing "s" stripped to name each repeated child element (so a key like <code class="font-mono text-xs" style="color:var(--accent)">tags</code> produces repeated <code class="font-mono text-xs" style="color:var(--accent)">{`<tag>`}</code> elements, one per item) instead of wrapping the whole array in a single container element. Any key that isn't a legal XML tag name (containing spaces, starting with a digit, etc.) falls back to a generic <code class="font-mono text-xs" style="color:var(--accent)">{`<item>`}</code> tag so the output always stays valid XML. Text content is escaped for the five XML special characters (<code class="font-mono text-xs" style="color:var(--accent)">&amp; &lt; &gt; " '</code>-equivalents) before being inserted.</p>
        </section>
        <section>
          <h2 class="mb-1.5 text-xs font-semibold uppercase tracking-wider" style="color:var(--accent)">Features</h2>
          <ul class="list-disc space-y-1 pl-5">
            <li>Configurable root element name via the toolbar's Root field</li>
            <li>Arrays expand into repeated sibling elements rather than a nested list wrapper</li>
            <li><code class="font-mono text-xs" style="color:var(--accent)">null</code> values render as self-closing empty tags</li>
            <li>Invalid tag-name keys automatically fall back to a generic <code class="font-mono text-xs" style="color:var(--accent)">{`<item>`}</code> element</li>
            <li>Full XML entity escaping on all text content</li>
            <li>Runs entirely client-side — nothing you paste is uploaded</li>
          </ul>
        </section>
        <section>
          <h2 class="mb-1.5 text-xs font-semibold uppercase tracking-wider" style="color:var(--accent)">Example</h2>
          <p>Input: <code class="font-mono text-xs" style="color:var(--accent)">{`{ "user": { "name": "Ana", "email": null }, "tags": ["dev", "admin"] }`}</code> with root name <code class="font-mono text-xs" style="color:var(--accent)">root</code>.</p>
          <p>Output:</p>
          <pre class="overflow-x-auto rounded border p-3 font-mono text-xs" style="border-color:var(--border);background:var(--bg)">{`<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<root>
  <user>
    <name>Ana</name>
    <email/>
  </user>
  <tag>dev</tag>
  <tag>admin</tag>
</root>`}</pre>
        </section>
        <section>
          <h2 class="mb-1.5 text-xs font-semibold uppercase tracking-wider" style="color:var(--accent)">Common errors</h2>
          <p>The array-key singularization is a naive trailing-"s" strip, not real linguistic singularization: a key like <code class="font-mono text-xs" style="color:var(--accent)">tags</code> becomes <code class="font-mono text-xs" style="color:var(--accent)">tag</code> correctly, but an irregular plural like <code class="font-mono text-xs" style="color:var(--accent)">children</code> would become <code class="font-mono text-xs" style="color:var(--accent)">childre</code>. If that matters for your output, rename the array's key in the source JSON before converting, or edit the generated tag names afterward. Also note that top-level JSON arrays (rather than an array nested inside an object) are converted item-by-item under a generic <code class="font-mono text-xs" style="color:var(--accent)">{`<item>`}</code> tag, since there's no parent key to derive a name from.</p>
        </section>
        <section>
          <h2 class="mb-1.5 text-xs font-semibold uppercase tracking-wider" style="color:var(--accent)">Best practices</h2>
          <p>Pick array key names that are already correct plain-English plurals ending in a single "s" (<code class="font-mono text-xs" style="color:var(--accent)">tags</code>, <code class="font-mono text-xs" style="color:var(--accent)">items</code>, <code class="font-mono text-xs" style="color:var(--accent)">users</code>) so the generated singular element names read naturally. If the target system needs attributes rather than child elements (e.g. <code class="font-mono text-xs" style="color:var(--accent)">{`<user id="1">`}</code> instead of a nested <code class="font-mono text-xs" style="color:var(--accent)">{`<id>`}</code> element), you'll need to restructure the JSON or post-process the XML, since this converter always emits child elements, never attributes.</p>
        </section>
      </div>
    </details>

    <FAQ items={faqItems} />
  </div>
</ToolLayout>
